---
import '../styles/global.css';
import SkipLink from '../shared/components/SkipLink.astro';
import Nav from '../shared/components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../shared/components/Footer.astro';

/**
 * Root layout. Every route renders through this.
 *
 * The direction contract below MUST remain the first child of <body> and
 * MUST survive the production build — it is the auditable record of the
 * visual world every component inherits. Do not move it into a child or
 * slotted component; Astro strips a slot's leading comment. An HTML comment
 * renders nothing, so SkipLink is still the first real element for keyboard
 * and screen-reader users despite following it in source.
 *
 * <main> owns the axis/measure (mx-auto max-w-stock), not each page. Nav and
 * Footer apply the same "axis" utility independently, so the registration
 * axis appears at the identical inset in all three landmarks — this is what
 * makes it a real site-wide alignment guide instead of per-page discipline
 * (the open item from Phase 3a's close-out). The three segments are not
 * required to paint as one unbroken stroke — a registration mark is a guide
 * every section references, not a single physical ruler — so normal spacing
 * between landmarks is fine.
 */
interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
}

const { title, description } = Astro.props;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <!--
      Progressive-enhancement flag for MenuPanel.astro (Phase 3c): synchronous
      and unprocessed (is:inline) so it runs before first paint, avoiding a
      frame where the menu panel is visible in its no-JS layout. Its absence
      is not an error state — it is the fallback docs/user-flows.md requires.
    -->
    <script is:inline>
      document.documentElement.classList.add('js');
    </script>
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />

    <!--
      All three faces are preloaded because all three are used above the fold,
      and a swap after first paint is exactly the flash the direction's tight
      stencil tracking makes most obvious. Space Mono is included on the
      contract's own terms: the FIRST VIEWPORT block below puts the lot/date
      stamp in the opening frame, and that is set in Space Mono. 63 KB total.
    -->
    <link
      rel="preload"
      href="/fonts/archivo-latin-var.woff2"
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
    />
    <link
      rel="preload"
      href="/fonts/archivo-narrow-700-latin.woff2"
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
    />
    <link
      rel="preload"
      href="/fonts/space-mono-400-latin.woff2"
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <!--
    THESIS: Four shipped projects presented as consigned goods with placards,
    each carrying its own lot code, consignee and stamped status. Refuses the
    dark developer-portfolio card grid and its cream-editorial opposite alike.

    OWN-WORLD: Achromatic stock (was kraft/manila; see AMENDED 2026-09-07
    below), black stencil ink, no accent — live/shipped status carries as a
    solid ink fill instead of colour. Condensed grotesque caps; monospace
    reserved for real codes only. Hazard-band rules, rubber-stamped status
    marks, lot numbers, consignee lines. One label stock across all 7 routes;
    a single vertical registration axis everything aligns to; density itself
    is tone, never decoration.

    STORY: A founder understands this person ships real things, believes they are
    genuinely live, and opens a project brief.

    FIRST VIEWPORT: Consignment placard top-left — GARY REYES, INDUSTRIAL
    ENGINEERING, DLSU, MANILA — set as a shipping mark against the registration
    axis, lot/date stamp opposite. Work begins immediately below as placarded
    units. Primary action: the first unit.

    AMENDED 2026-09-07 (a): counts only (six->four projects, 11->7 routes,
    case study->brief), to match the 2026-09-04 scope revision. No part of
    the assigned direction was altered.

    AMENDED 2026-09-07 (b): the safety-orange accent is removed, not
    recoloured — Gary's explicit call, made after being told this is one of
    the single most common developer-portfolio palettes and would work
    against this build's own "must not look like every other dev portfolio"
    guardrail. Unlike amendment (a), this DOES alter the assigned direction;
    recorded honestly rather than folded into the first note. Everything the
    direction actually owes structurally — the manifest metaphor, the
    registration axis, one continuous stock, density as tone — is unchanged.
    Full reasoning: PROJECT_FACTS.md "Visual direction".

    FORM: Shipping manifest / logistics labeling; candidate 3 of 7; seed d4e5136b.

    FINISH: unreviewed and undocumented is unfinished; this build ends with the
    finish review, the verdict, and DESIGN.md
    -->
    <SkipLink />
    <Nav />
    <main id="main-content" class="axis max-w-stock mx-auto">
      <slot />
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>
